beads.description:
`NotifyInitialStatus` (`1 << 14`, declared in `ipn/backend.go`) makes the first Notify of a session carry a whole `ipnstate.Status` in `Notify.InitialStatus`. Upstream's doc comment on the bit is a recommendation as much as a description: paired with subsequent `Notify.SelfChange` and `Notify.PeersChanged` it lets a watcher stitch together a continuous view of the node "without fetching the netmap directly", and new code is told to prefer it over `LocalClient.NetMap` because it is a stable, client-facing snapshot type.

The interesting part is the build order in `WatchNotificationsAs` (`ipn/ipnlocal/local.go`), which is the part to port rather than re-invent. The engine half is built FIRST, before `b.mu` is taken:

    var statusSB *ipnstate.StatusBuilder
    if mask&ipn.NotifyInitialStatus != 0 {
        statusSB = &ipnstate.StatusBuilder{WantPeers: true}
        b.e.UpdateStatus(statusSB)
    }

and the comment above it gives the reason: `b.e.UpdateStatus` has its own locking, and the lock ordering is outer-to-inner backend-then-engine, never the reverse. The backend half is then filled under `b.mu` (`b.updateStatusLocked(statusSB); ini.InitialStatus = statusSB.Status()`) in the same critical section that registers the session in `b.notifyWatchers`, so the snapshot is assembled atomically with the subscription and no event can be delivered to this watcher before its snapshot is.

This fork already builds the exact analogue and already worries about the same hazard. `Backend::status()` (`src/ipn/mod.rs`) returns a `StatusReport` -- state, want_running, self addresses, MagicDNS suffix, active exit node, peers, health -- and `Request::Status` hands it straight back. It queries the engine's netmap only when `Running`, and it bounds `dev.status()` with `STATUS_QUERY_TIMEOUT` precisely so the backend lock is not held indefinitely. What is missing is the ability to ASK for it on the bus: `Request::Watch` has `initial_state`, `initial_netmap`, `prefs` and `policy`, and no status. So a watcher that wants both a snapshot and a stream opens two LocalAPI connections, and there is no ordering guarantee between them -- the stream can deliver a transition that the snapshot request has not yet observed, or vice versa, and the client cannot tell which happened.

DECISIONS. FIRST, the field: emit the existing `StatusReport` as a new optional `NotifyView` field rather than minting a reduced projection, so `watch` and `status` cannot drift. SECOND, the ordering guarantee is the whole point of the bit and has to be reproduced in this daemon's own idiom: `stream_notify` currently front-loads the daemon-built prefs and policy frames BEFORE the first device epoch is derived, which is the right place for a status front-load too -- but `Backend::status()` awaits `dev.status()`, so decide deliberately whether to take the snapshot before subscribing to the engine bus (simplest, and can miss a transition that lands in between) or to subscribe first and buffer (faithful, and more machinery than this bead needs). Go gets atomicity from one mutex; this daemon cannot, and the honest answer may be to take the snapshot first and document the window. Say which, in the field's rustdoc. THIRD, `WantPeers: true` is not incidental -- Go's initial status includes peers -- so do not ship a peerless snapshot and call it this bit.

Upstream used to drop notifications for a slow watcher and now refuses to. This fork does what upstream stopped doing, and the difference is not a nicety -- it is the difference between a subscriber that knows its view is stale and one that does not.

`sendToLocked` (`ipn/ipnlocal/local.go`) writes each per-session frame into a channel created 128 deep (`ch := make(chan *ipn.Notify, 128)`) with a non-blocking send, and the `default` arm is a disconnect:

    select {
    case sess.ch <- nForSess:
    default:
        if sess.mask&ipn.NotifyInProcessNoDisconnect != 0 {
            select {
            case sess.ch <- nForSess:
            case <-sess.ctx.Done():
            }
            continue
        }
        b.closeLaggingWatchSessionLocked(sess)
    }

`closeLaggingWatchSessionLocked` removes the session from `b.notifyWatchers`, DRAINS everything still queued -- the comment gives the reason, "the session already fell behind, so the queued delta stream is not trustworthy" -- replaces it with a single terminal frame whose `ErrMessage` is the constant `watchIPNBusFellBehindMessage` (`"IPN bus consumer fell behind; closing watch"`), and closes the channel. A LocalAPI watcher that cannot keep up is therefore told exactly once, and disconnected, and knows it must re-subscribe and re-snapshot. The blocking alternative is reserved for `NotifyInProcessNoDisconnect`, which is in-process only and which the LocalAPI handler refuses outright.

This fork's `stream_notify` (`src/server.rs`) reads the engine's bus, and the engine's `deliver` (`ts_runtime/src/ipn_bus.rs` at the pinned engine rev) treats a full 128-deep channel as success:

    match tx.try_send(n) {
        Ok(()) => false,
        Err(mpsc::error::TrySendError::Full(_)) => false,
        Err(mpsc::error::TrySendError::Closed(_)) => true,
    }

Its own comment cites Go's non-blocking send as the model -- which it faithfully was, before upstream replaced the drop with a disconnect. The result today is the worse half of both designs: a watcher whose reader is slow (a `tnet status --watch` piped into a stalled pager, a management agent doing synchronous work per frame) loses frames one at a time, keeps the connection open, and diverges from the daemon permanently with no signal on either side.

This one splits, and the split is the point of filing it. The DAEMON half is small and buildable now: `NotifyView::error` already exists, `stream_notify` owns the socket, so emitting one terminal frame and returning is a few lines. The ENGINE half is not: the daemon cannot know a drop occurred, because the engine discards the notification without recording anything. That needs a lag signal out of `IpnBusWatcher` -- a dropped-count, or a `Lagged` variant on the watcher's item type, in the shape of `tokio::sync::broadcast::error::RecvError::Lagged`.

DECISIONS. FIRST, file the engine ask before writing daemon code; a daemon-side heuristic that guesses at lag (a write timeout, a queue-depth estimate) would be a facsimile and is exactly what the honest-omission rule exists to prevent. SECOND, the daemon-built feeds need nothing and should be stated as out of scope in the same breath: prefs and policy ride `tokio::sync::watch`, which coalesces to the latest value instead of dropping an entry, and for a full-snapshot feed coalescing loses nothing. Only the engine `mpsc` bus has the hazard. THIRD, when the signal exists, port Go's ORDER exactly -- drain, then one terminal frame, then close -- because a client that receives stale queued frames after the error message cannot tell which side of the gap they came from.
